in this episode of cloud performance  atlas I help some music masters with  their connection quota will we hit the  high notes stay tuned to find out  Meister was adopting the latest and  greatest in serverless technology and  had recently moved some of their systems  from compute engine to cloud functions  sadly one challenge popped up as the new  function started to eclipse their  connection quota but wait a minute  connection quota see most likely your  GCF calls will be doing a lot of  external RPC calls uh querying API is  fetching proxy resources etc etc each  outbound request is a connection and  there's a quota involved with it so if  each one of your functions is doing ten  RPC calls and you're running a million  instances quota might get in your way  thankfully GCF has a little feature that  allows you to reuse connections between  invitations of a cloud function if  you're fetching from the same host you  don't have to pay the extra overhead of  their new connection since it's already  opened for example code like this was  being used by Meister to fetch an asset  from one of their databases although  this code is pretty standard the HTTP  request has no persistence which means  that it performs a new connection on  every function invocation we can fix  this and maintain persistent connections  by using a custom HTTP agent with me  keep alive option after running a small  test the cloud functions API quota page  in the cloud console shows a huge  improvement between the two code paths  and also has the side effect of  decreasing DNS resolve switch by the way  is also something you have to provision  quota for now in my opinion one of the  strongest things about GCF is its  out-of-the-box support and helper  libraries to the rest of Google Cloud  however because those api's generally  make RPC calls through socket  connections we can run into the same  problem with connection quota for  example when using the cloud pub/sub api  their code was using one connection in  two DNS queries per invocation  however by moving the pub/sub variable  to a global state and declaring it Const  we can remove unnecessary connections  and DNS queries re sharing the  connection with these simple changes  Meister was able to get an 8x reduction  in connection and DNS usage to learn  more about our changes check out the  article linked in the description  below and don't forget to check out the  rest of the cloud performance Atlas  content because when it comes to  performance every millisecond counts
 

